{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The argument claims the large service's formally verified isolation domains provide a 'mathematical guarantee of independence' that makes it strictly more resilient. But formal verification of a large monolithic service cannot guarantee independence against shared causes originating OUTSIDE its specified threat model — a limitation SRC_001 itself concedes (countercondition 3). The very problem asks for evidence distinguishing truly independent failures from those merely appearing uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. By admitting the threat model may not capture all relevant shared causes, the claim's own counterconditions refute its central assertion: the large service's formal independence is conditional on the completeness of its threat model, which can never be established absolutely. Thus 'strictly more resilient' is unwarranted — the large service's guarantees hold only within a circumscribed model, while novel shared causes (precisely the distinguishing failure modes the problem asks about) can collapse both architectures. The claim fails to show that formal verification within a bounded threat model is epistemically stronger than empirical uncorrelation when the unmodeled failure space is what the problem targets.","counterexample":null}]}